00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:30.870
that have their offices in Brazil as well.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:36.230
And the first time I moved to a more, let's say, agile way of

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:40.110
working was when I moved from my hometown, Porto Alegre in the

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.110
south of Brazil to a startup in Florianópolis, also in the south of

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:47.750
Brazil, one of the most beautiful cities in Brazil, if I can say.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:56.150
And in this company, we were around 20 people, around 12 developers.

00:08:56.710 --> 00:09:03.400
And it's kind of funny because I was hired as the second tester of

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:07.800
this company because the other tester that they have there was

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.760
working for two years and he couldn't take vacation because he was the

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:15.800
only one that knew how to write the automated tests.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:17.960
And so he trained me.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:19.240
And that's, I'm going to interrupt for a second.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.040
That's a problem into itself, right?

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:24.480
If you're ever in a position where you can't take a vacation,

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:26.880
something over overarching is wrong.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Anyway, please continue.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:29.080
Yeah.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.520
So he trained me so he could take vacation afterwards.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.800
He went on vacation.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:38.320
He came back and I thought to myself, what if this guy lives,

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.800
we might be able to go on vacations as well.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:47.580
So I decided to change the approach to not be the only one doing testing.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:54.620
So one of the approaches that I used, so we were doing test

00:09:54.620 --> 00:10:01.220
automation using Cucumber in a programming language that was not

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:04.620
the language that the developers were used to write code.

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:10.340
And at that time, there was a new test framework coming up.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:14.500
We were starting a project using the same technology.

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:19.700
And then I decided, what if we move for this specific project to this

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:23.460
other framework that is in the same language that the developers are already

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:30.560
used to, and then we started experimenting with it and we start.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:36.180
And I started doing knowledge sharing sessions, pair programming, pair

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:39.100
testing with the developers, coding dojos.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:42.620
So I could go on vacation and they could take care of the

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:44.220
tasks when I was not there.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.700
I would say it was a success.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:51.380
I worked for this company for a couple of years before I moved

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:56.180
from Brazil to Europe and they never hired a QA anymore.

00:10:57.340 --> 00:11:00.580
And they still build very high quality software.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:01.100
When was this?

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:01.940
What year was this?

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.420
It was from 2014 until 2016.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:09.820
Which is great because what's interesting is that is right around

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:15.820
the time the A-B testing podcast first started, but that is

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:17.900
principle number seven in action.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:21.340
You spread information across the team so you don't need

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:23.460
specialists to bottleneck the system.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:24.900
So that's incredible.

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:29.020
