It's my podcast, dammit.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:19.510
I I I would love to have that courage to leave.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:21.150
And I think I'm kind of setting myself up.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:24.630
I have a job that requires, you know, I'm an air quote relocation.

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:27.390
I made I would not move.

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:29.470
I would get temporary housing and try it out for a walk.

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:32.830
So I'm not going to sell myself to that.

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:35.230
And also, I would negotiate for more remote work.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.730
But I'm curious.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:39.570
So that's that's I'm going to do a lot of due diligence.

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.330
Now, I'm going to track down employees that work at these

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:43.610
whatever company I decided to go to and really figure out what it's like

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:44.290
before I go there.

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.810
I want to make sure I can do what I do and do what I do best

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:49.690
safely and in a good environment where I can actually have the impact

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:53.050
I want to have. If that fails, I want to be not afraid to say,

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:54.210
you know what? Nope.

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:57.930
I'm going to try again and, you know, take whatever comes back with that.

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:03.610
So the question I have is I want to know more about like what we're

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:07.730
one, what were the things that really what was the tipping point

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:10.010
that really made you say no, really noped you out of there.

00:17:10.370 --> 00:17:13.130
And then two, if you could do it again and like there are some due

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:17.170
diligence, like during the interview or during the lead up to taking that job.

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:20.050
What would you do differently now?

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:23.490
Well, actually, it was a little bit of my fault as well.

00:17:24.370 --> 00:17:28.650
So the main thing is that I've been working remotely

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:31.410
since before the pandemic.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:35.490
So when I was still living in the Netherlands, I worked for GitLab,

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:37.730
which started as a remote company.

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:43.330
I joined them when they were already like a big company for a pre IPO.

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:50.050
And I got used to working remotely and I continued working remotely

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:52.050
all other gigs that I worked for.

00:17:54.030 --> 00:18:00.290
And in the Netherlands, there was a time that I decided to quit my job

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:07.330
when I was at GitLab for a year and a half to work on my own my own business.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:12.690
I am a kind of an entrepreneur, have a company with just myself.

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:17.050
When you were saying that I might work for myself, I might work

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:18.930
for someone else, for a company.

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:20.410
I do both nowadays.

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:22.730
I have my own company, I have my own clients and I have

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:25.890
my own job as well, like as an employee.

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:33.250
So I quit my job at GitLab, spent like a kind of a sabbatical year

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:40.450
thinking about life while building my own business and trying things

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:43.040
and failing and adjusting.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.800
I want to live here.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.320
I like the way you're living your life.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:47.280
I just want to say that.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:50.640
Traveling, living abroad, taking a year off to think about

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:53.200
what you want to do, having a balance between your own stuff

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:54.240
and the corporate stuff.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:55.040
I like it.

00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:00.160
And I would say that it's like a partnership between me and my wife.

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:04.160
She was dependent of my visa for a while.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:07.360
I had to be dependent of her visa for a while as well.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:08.800
We are Brazilians.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:09.520
We live in Europe.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:13.040
We don't have a European citizenship.

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:16.640
So we depend on a working visa to stay in Europe still.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:20.560
Nowadays, we have applied for Spanish citizenship, which is

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:24.320
probably going to come this year, but we still depend on a visa.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:29.040
So this is really a partnership between the both of us.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:38.540
So I decided that I want to have a sabbatical to work on my own stuff

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:41.740
and think about what I was going to do afterwards.

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:47.340
Worked for a company in the Netherlands before moving to Spain

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:49.580
for about five months.

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:55.900
And then I decided to leave because we wanted to move again.

00:19:55.900 --> 00:20:00.940
So we lived in Oslo, in Norway, then in the Netherlands, in Amsterdam.

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:05.740
And there were three things that we did not like.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:07.500
One, the weather.

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:08.380
We are Brazilians.

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:09.980
We have very good weather in Brazil.

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:17.790
We got not used to the weather, nor in Oslo,

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:20.270
neither in Amsterdam.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:28.350
The language is a great barrier for you to feel part of the country you live.

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:32.430
I speak a little bit of Norwegian, but I can't communicate.

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:35.310
I can't have a conversation with someone that speaks fluently

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:36.030
in Norwegian.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:39.710
I can just read some things and introduce myself,

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:41.630
but I can't make a conversation.

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.990
I never learned Norwegian Dutch.

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:47.950
It's a very difficult language.

00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:50.350
So we decided that we wanted to move to a place

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:55.890
that we would have a good weather,

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:59.170
culture that is more similar to us,

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:01.970
and also a language that we speak.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:04.930
So we came to Spain where we have the three things.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:07.970
My wife, we speak Spanish.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:09.410
We have a Latin culture.

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:10.210
We are Latins.

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:14.850
And we have a very good weather here in Barcelona where we live.

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:21.310
And in Barcelona, I started working for Typeform at first.

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:25.070
I worked for them with them for almost a year.

00:21:26.350 --> 00:21:29.790
And my role at Typeform was quality assistance,

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:31.550
assistant.

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:34.590
So we had a team of four quality assistants,

00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:40.270
and we were basically assisting many developer teams

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:43.870
that have different needs in terms of quality.

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.790
Some of them need a hand with test automation.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:50.510
Some of them were doing test automation just great,

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:56.220
and we would give them a hand with, I don't know.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:59.740
There was a case where we were using

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:05.500
a very expensive SAS for doing visual regression testing.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:08.140
So we decided to experiment with open source solutions

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:10.140
that we could host on our own

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:12.780
and replace the very expensive one.

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:13.740
So we did that.

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:17.260
We were doing trainings and that kind of thing.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:19.900
And then there were layoffs.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:24.460
The quality team was dismantled.

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:25.900
And I decided, you know what?

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.620
I'm going to go back to what I did in the Netherlands,

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:31.660
focus on my own job, on my own business.

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:34.620
And I did that for about two years.

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:39.500
In these two years that I, like when you do,

00:22:40.660 --> 00:22:43.700
like most of my clients, they are from Brazil,

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.620
and I live in Europe.

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:48.980
And the Brazilian currency is very low

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:52.340
if you make the change between Brazilian reais and euros.

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:55.300
So you have to get quite a big amount of money

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:58.340
to have clients in Brazil to live in Europe.

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:01.620
So I was experimenting with different things,

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:04.100
like my online courses were giving me

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:05.860
some part of my revenue.

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:07.540
At the same time, I was experimenting

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:10.820
with different kind of services that I could offer,

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:13.940
doing a little bit of consulting for a few clients.

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:19.140
So one thing that I did, which I did already five classes,

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:23.540
was a mentorship called Test Design Masterclass

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:30.260
where I teach software design applied to test automation,

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:31.380
to software testing.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:36.340
This masterclass, I teach more than 40 students

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:39.540
on this already, and it was inspired by this book here

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:40.500
that I'm going to show you.

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:43.620
It's called A Philosophy of Software Design.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:44.580
I have not read that one.

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:49.460
And I love the cover, which is kind of spaghetti coat on top.

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:52.180
Then you apply the philosophy of software design,

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:55.140
and then you have very well decoupled software in the bottom.

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:57.860
I'd like to point out for our listeners

00:23:57.860 --> 00:24:00.260
that there are very few books on software I haven't read.

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:01.940
There probably are a lot, but I haven't read that one.

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:02.740
It sounds like it's a good one.

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:04.020
I'll put it on my list.

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:05.460
I highly recommend it.

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:07.060
It's from John Osterhout.

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:10.980
He was a professor at the CES course in Stanford.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:17.860
And he basically wrote the book as what he was doing

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:20.340
at Stanford, the university.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.500
So he was teaching software design

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:27.300
in a very different way that other universities were doing.

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:35.220
So he would give to his students a kind of a softer product

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:36.660
that they had to build.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:38.020
And they would build it for...

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:43.780
So they would have classes on software design,

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:46.100
but they would have this exercise

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:48.180
of building an application on their own.

00:24:48.740 --> 00:24:51.140
And they would send to him for review,

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:55.300
and he would give feedback so they could apply

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:59.780
the principles of software design while they were learning.

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:03.540
And they would have a few rounds of building

00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:07.700
and receiving feedback and updating the code

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:08.740
based on the feedback.

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:10.500
And in the second round, they would not send

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:13.380
just for the professor to review,

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:15.780
but also to the other peers.

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:18.660
So I decided to copy what he was doing

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:22.180
teaching software design, but applied to software testing.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:24.100
So I built a web application,

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:32.430
and I gave to my students the exercise

00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:36.590
of you have to write tests for this application.

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:38.430
You have to write end-to-end tests.

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:40.990
You have to write tests for the API.

00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:44.910
You have to write tests for the specific components

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:46.430
of the front end.

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:49.470
You have to write accessibility tests

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:53.310
for the things to make sure the application is accessible.

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:58.190
And depending on the level of seniority of the students,

00:25:58.190 --> 00:26:02.350
they would have more difficult or easier exercises.

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:04.750
If they were very senior,

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:06.270
they could even apply that

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:08.590
to doing continuous integration as well.

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:13.630
It's been a very nice experiment now.

00:26:14.270 --> 00:26:19.550
I'm going to go with the sixth class in June this year.

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:24.770
But after two years just working on my own

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:26.850
for the first year, it worked pretty well.

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:32.210
The second year, it wasn't doing as well as I imagined.

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:35.650
And then I decided to go back to the market.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:40.690
And then I was very selective on finding a new job.

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:42.370
I got this one that I mentioned.

00:26:44.060 --> 00:26:47.100
And now I realize that I am telling all that

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:49.340
because of the question you asked about the job.

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:51.740
Yeah, but before you go on, as we do,

00:26:51.740 --> 00:26:54.140
I have two follow-up questions before we get to the end point.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.820
I love the journey here because, again,

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:00.060
it underscores that the principles

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.300
that Brendan and I have talked about are not things...

00:27:02.300 --> 00:27:03.900
It's not like we invented them and said,

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:04.780
these are going to happen.

00:27:04.780 --> 00:27:06.220
We just put some labels on things

00:27:06.220 --> 00:27:07.420
that were already happening.

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:09.260
It's great to hear this happen independently.

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:10.860
Two questions.

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:11.980
One's sort of an easy one.

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:14.860
You mentioned at a company you had four quality assistants

00:27:14.860 --> 00:27:16.780
and their term quality assistant

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:18.780
I think originally came from Atlassian.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:19.900
I don't know the name exactly.

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:21.340
They wanted people to help.

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:23.420
They wanted quality folks to help developers

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:24.300
do better testing.

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:26.540
So I understand your journey.

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:28.620
I'm just curious, the other folks

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:31.820
that were quality assistants or quality advisors,

00:27:31.820 --> 00:27:33.580
quality coaches, whatever you want to call them,

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:38.220
was that a role they also grew in independently?

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:39.900
Did they have to kind of be taught to do that?

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:41.980
Did they go there because they saw the light of it?

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:44.220
Were they reluctant to do that?

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:46.540
And I ask for a reason,

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:48.380
but I'm just curious to have to describe

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:49.260
every single one of them.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:52.780
But briefly, what was their journey to this role

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:53.740
and how did they receive it?

00:27:54.620 --> 00:28:00.500
Actually, the company didn't have this role before.

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:04.660
They were like normal quality engineers in the beginning.

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:07.780
So they were transitioning to quality assistants

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:11.380
and they hired me when they were in this transition.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:13.700
So besides helping the developers,

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:18.900
I was also helping my other peers as quality assistants

00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:24.740
to grow into this kind of model of working.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:25.380
How did that go?

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.780
It went pretty well, I would say,

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:32.540
especially for the youngest one,

00:28:32.540 --> 00:28:36.060
which was not as senior as the other two,

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:39.260
because he was more open-minded.

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:41.500
The other one, they had already their issues.

00:28:41.500 --> 00:28:42.460
I'm not surprised.

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:44.700
I think the folks that have been in traditional

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:48.060
versus modern, traditional testing for a long time,

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:50.700
they want to hold onto those roots a little tighter

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:55.820
and moving to giving someone else that expertise

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:57.340
can be difficult for them.

00:28:57.340 --> 00:29:02.460
They want to hold on to the notion

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:06.940
that they have the right mindset to do it properly

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:09.580
and nobody else does, which often not all,

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:10.780
a lot of folks will have.

00:29:10.780 --> 00:29:12.620
I have a funny story about Microsoft.

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:13.580
Really quickly, when I first worked there,

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:15.020
I worked on networking on Windows 95

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:16.540
and we had a guy there who was a network,

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:17.900
a network admin.

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:19.500
I did a lot of work to make network work.

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:24.380
He was asked to share some of his knowledge with us

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:25.980
so we could all do better network testing

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:28.300
and he refused because he said,

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:29.820
I took certification in classes.

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:31.740
I shouldn't have to share this information,

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:33.900
which is a horrible, horrible approach.

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:35.820
And he was escrowed out of the building the next day.

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:39.740
It was fine, but information sharing is great.

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:42.640
So as people move into these roles,

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:43.840
I worry a little bit that,

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:45.840
I mean, there is a tough transition.

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:47.600
You have to have some sort of leadership support.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:49.040
The company wanted to move that direction.

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:50.960
So it makes sense.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:53.200
The other question, and I'm always curious.

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:54.160
But if I can interrupt,

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:56.880
I would say that the other two,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:00.880
they were not reluctant completely,

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:05.600
but simply the youngest one was more open-minded.

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:08.320
The other one, we complement each other very well.

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:10.880
So there were things that they didn't like to do

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:13.200
and they would do while there were things

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:14.880
that they were very great on doing.

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:17.120
Like teaching disseminating knowledge.

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:19.680
So we would complement each other very well.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:20.320
I would say.

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:20.640
That's fair.

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:21.760
Thanks for the clarification on that.

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:22.480
That makes sense.

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:24.720
The other question I have, unrelated.

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:27.120
I talked about your test design masterclass.

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:30.800
There's whole books on test design

00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:32.000
that are mostly useless.

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:35.040
The trick with test design,

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:36.240
I just talked to some folks

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:38.800
at a company I'm working with

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:42.300
about test design yesterday.

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:43.100
And the challenge is,

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:45.980
if you have, for example, Chad GPT,

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:47.500
here's an application, Chad GPT,

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.100
please generate me a list of test cases.

00:30:50.290 --> 00:30:52.050
It will generate you a list of test cases

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:54.050
as requested and they'll be fine.

00:30:54.050 --> 00:30:56.770
And they'll probably be the test cases

00:30:56.770 --> 00:30:59.250
that most testers would generate.

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:00.530
And that's great.

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:02.610
Well, it isn't great because

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:06.960
the trick is in test design

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:09.280
to design not just the tests well,

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:10.720
but design the right tests.

00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:14.160
And so much of testing,

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:15.760
whether it's developer testing

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:17.120
or by dedicated testing,

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:20.240
will over test some parts of the application

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:23.600
and under test some other parts

00:31:23.600 --> 00:31:24.320
of the application.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:25.360
That's where principle five,

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:26.640
we use data to help figure out

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:28.240
where the worst in those places may be.

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:30.480
But I'm just curious in your approach

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:32.240
to test design, maybe just give us

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:35.600
a little high level abstract or synopsis.

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:37.200
What is your approach to test design?

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:41.360
And is it does it coincide with the norms

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:42.320
a little off the tracks?

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:43.280
Is there a new approach?

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:44.800
I'm just kind of curious

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:48.240
because this is a even today

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:49.840
where we have developers doing

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:52.560
most of the testing in many teams.

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:54.480
Test design is still a challenge.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:56.800
So I'm just curious if you can just

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:58.240
just this is a nice little promo

00:31:58.240 --> 00:31:59.120
for your course as well.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:00.960
At a high level,

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:02.160
what is your approach to that?

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:06.400
So what I try to do is pass away

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:09.520
everything that I learned from the moment

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:11.360
that I started working as a tester

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.840
until the current moment.

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:17.840
So I kind of invented 14 principles

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:18.720
of test design.

00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:21.760
I can list them to you if you don't mind.

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:24.560
The first one is the description

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:26.000
of the suite, the sub suite

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:27.200
and the test cases,

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:29.360
which is more about what you just said,

00:32:29.360 --> 00:32:31.360
thinking about what test cases

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:34.080
make sense for the thing you were testing.

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:35.680
Then I talk about test levels,

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:38.320
like the unit testing,

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:40.320
the tests in the top of the pyramid,

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:41.440
the integration testing,

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:42.720
that kind of things.

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:44.480
I talk about abstractions.

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:46.240
I talk about assertions.

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:48.240
I talk about independence,

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:49.760
how tests should be independent

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:51.360
of each other.

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:53.280
I talk about infrastructure

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:54.720
and environments for running

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:56.160
the tests against.

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:58.640
I talk about tools, accessibility,

00:32:58.640 --> 00:33:00.400
testability, security,

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:02.800
the size of your tests,

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:05.280
the conventions you should follow,

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:08.320
the arrange, act, assert pattern.

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:09.600
And I talk also about

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:11.760
performance optimizations.

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:14.720
So I give them kind of lecture

00:33:14.720 --> 00:33:16.320
about these different topics.

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:16.960
In the beginning,

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:19.440
in the first two classes,

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:22.240
I would give them in the first hour.

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:26.560
So it's like one month classes

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:29.280
on the Saturdays where I would,

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:30.000
in the first hour,

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:30.960
give them lectures.

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:31.920
And in the second time,

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:33.760
it would focus more on the exercises.

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:34.800
And then they would,

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:36.480
after the class,

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:38.640
have to do the exercises on their own.

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:41.120
They would have like four or five days

00:33:41.120 --> 00:33:43.120
to do the work and send for my review.

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:44.880
So I would review and they would have,

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:47.920
they would come back with the feedback.

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:50.820
After, in the third one,

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:54.180
I decided that I spend too much time

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:56.500
lecturing about test design.

00:33:56.500 --> 00:33:58.980
So I prerecorded the lessons

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:00.980
and I would give them a free course

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:03.700
teaching exactly what I would.

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:06.740
So we would spend more time on practical stuff.

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:08.100
And actually, as an aside,

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:08.660
there's a whole,

00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:13.440
in the U.S. where education is poor

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:14.320
to start off with,

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:15.200
there are some folks trying

00:34:15.200 --> 00:34:15.680
to make it better.

00:34:15.680 --> 00:34:16.800
And one of the techniques they use

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:18.640
is to give students the lecture

00:34:18.640 --> 00:34:19.680
to take home

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:20.960
and then use the in-person time

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:21.680
with the instructor

00:34:21.680 --> 00:34:23.040
to actually get practical advice

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:23.840
and to walk through stuff

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:24.960
and answer questions.

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:26.480
I think it's very effective.

00:34:27.680 --> 00:34:29.120
And it was a learning for me.

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:31.920
It was like something that I realized

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:34.880
that we could spend more time

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:37.360
like with asking questions

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:38.480
and answering them

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:40.480
while they would have

00:34:41.360 --> 00:34:42.640
the live time with me.

00:34:43.200 --> 00:34:44.880
And then they could take

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:46.400
the lectures on their own.

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:52.620
So the way it works,

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:54.380
it's like through code reviews.

00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:55.980
So they have to create,

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:57.660
so they have to fork the project

00:34:57.660 --> 00:34:59.820
on GitHub, work on,

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:02.060
clone their own fork

00:35:02.060 --> 00:35:03.580
on their own machines,

00:35:03.580 --> 00:35:04.780
do the exercises,

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:05.980
create pull requests

00:35:05.980 --> 00:35:06.940
so it could review.

00:35:07.820 --> 00:35:09.580
And then I would give them

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:11.180
very constructive feedback.

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:11.980
And the feedback

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:13.020
that I would give them

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:15.900
is it would be always related

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:18.140
to one of these 14 principles

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:19.260
that I just mentioned.

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:23.100
And I would tell why they are

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:27.410
not following the principle,

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:29.940
what was the problem

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:32.020
of not following the principle

00:35:32.020 --> 00:35:34.100
and how they could apply

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:35.380
the principle

00:35:35.380 --> 00:35:37.300
so they would really learn

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:38.420
and they would be able

00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:40.020
to apply the principle

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:41.540
in a second round.

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:42.420
And in the last,

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:44.340
in the final class,

00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:46.980
I show them my solution.

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:48.500
This is how I would solve.

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:50.020
This is how I would do

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:50.900
all the exercise.

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:52.020
And then we spend

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:52.980
a couple of hours

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:53.780
where I show them

00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:57.460
all the test cases

00:35:57.540 --> 00:35:58.180
that I wrote

00:35:58.180 --> 00:35:59.380
in the different levels

00:35:59.380 --> 00:36:00.180
and I explained

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:01.060
the different things

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:02.260
and they can use that

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:03.060
as a reference.

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:08.060
And so that's a little bit

00:36:08.060 --> 00:36:09.820
about the test design

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:10.940
master class.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:11.740
No, that's natural.

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:12.620
I recommend it.

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:13.420
Please send a link

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:15.180
with the other links

00:36:15.180 --> 00:36:15.820
you're going to send me.

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:17.500
Of course.

00:36:17.500 --> 00:36:17.820
All right.

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:18.540
So here we are.

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:20.380
We are a half hour-ish in,

00:36:20.380 --> 00:36:21.420
a little more.

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:23.500
And now lots of background,

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:24.140
which is great.

00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:25.180
And lots of interjections

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:26.620
for me, not so great.

00:36:26.620 --> 00:36:28.300
But what was the question

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:28.860
I asked?

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:29.980
You'll remember better than I can.

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:30.860
Go ahead and answer it.

00:36:30.860 --> 00:36:32.540
It was related to me

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:33.260
leaving the job

00:36:33.260 --> 00:36:33.980
in the first day.

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:35.100
Yes, yes.

00:36:35.100 --> 00:36:37.500
So after a while,

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:38.860
I decided, you know what?

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:39.900
I want to go back

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:42.540
to work as an employee.

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:46.060
But still having my own company

00:36:46.060 --> 00:36:47.660
and I started looking for jobs.

00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:49.180
And one of the things that

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:50.380
I thought I was ready

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:51.740
to go back to the office.

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:54.700
So I was looking for,

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:55.500
so as I said,

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:56.380
I am very selective.

00:36:56.380 --> 00:36:57.500
So I was looking for

00:36:58.060 --> 00:36:59.820
the work that it would be

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:02.780
in a domain that I wanted to work on

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:03.820
with the technology

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:05.020
that I feel comfortable.

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:07.100
But not just because

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:08.140
I feel comfortable,

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:09.020
but because I know

00:37:09.020 --> 00:37:09.820
I would be able

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:11.100
to contribute a lot.

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:18.420
But I thought I would be ready

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:20.020
to go back to the office.

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:22.690
And then when I arrived there

00:37:22.690 --> 00:37:23.490
in the first day,

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:25.250
I realized that I wasn't.

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:26.930
It was like a very small office

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:27.490
with like,

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:29.180
I don't know,

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:31.490
10 or 20 guys

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:34.130
all in the same small office

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:35.250
in a cool working place

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:36.290
here in Barcelona.

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:39.570
So this is one of the things

00:37:39.570 --> 00:37:40.770
that I felt that

00:37:40.770 --> 00:37:42.450
I was actually not ready

00:37:42.450 --> 00:37:43.730
to go back to the office.

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:45.250
At least not every day.

00:37:45.250 --> 00:37:46.690
I wouldn't mind going

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:48.850
once a week or twice maybe.

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:52.210
But having to be there every day,

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:54.610
it was kind of something

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:56.850
that I thought I would be ready,

00:37:56.850 --> 00:37:57.570
but I wasn't.

00:37:57.570 --> 00:37:59.010
And the other thing is that,

00:37:59.730 --> 00:38:00.530
and this is something

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:03.010
that was actually something

00:38:03.010 --> 00:38:04.530
that I even mentioned

00:38:05.570 --> 00:38:07.090
during the interview process.

00:38:07.090 --> 00:38:09.490
And they said that it wouldn't happen,

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:11.090
but it actually happened

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:12.450
is that half of the team

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:15.490
they were Germans and Austrians.

00:38:15.490 --> 00:38:17.170
And they would be speaking German

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:18.610
during the work time.

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:20.290
And I felt completely lost.

00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:23.010
And I wanted to be

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:25.410
in a more diverse place

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:27.330
where I would have

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:29.010
people from different backgrounds.

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:29.970
But we would always

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:31.170
speak in the same language.

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:33.410
So I would feel part.

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:35.730
So I came to Barcelona

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:36.610
to be in a place

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:38.930
where I understand what people say.

00:38:38.930 --> 00:38:40.530
And then I would get a job

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:41.730
where I wasn't understanding

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:42.930
what my colleagues were saying.

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.330
So this was the second thing

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:47.010
that made me leave.

00:38:47.570 --> 00:38:49.490
And then I decided,

00:38:49.490 --> 00:38:50.770
and then I continued

00:38:50.770 --> 00:38:51.970
searching for jobs.

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:53.170
And now I'm working

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:54.930
as a senior QA engineer

00:38:54.930 --> 00:38:56.530
in a company here in Barcelona.

00:38:57.250 --> 00:39:00.450
My role is senior QA engineer

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:03.360
slash QA advisor.

00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:05.440
I like that second half.

00:39:06.400 --> 00:39:07.200
Talk to me about that.

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:08.240
Do you do more of the first

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:09.040
or more of the second?

00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:12.320
So this is how it works.

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:14.720
I work in a company

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:18.000
where we develop different products,

00:39:18.640 --> 00:39:20.320
be them mobile products

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:22.000
or web products,

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:24.480
different teams they have there.

00:39:24.480 --> 00:39:27.920
It's like as if each product

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:30.000
was like a mini startup.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:32.320
They have their own stack.

00:39:32.320 --> 00:39:34.160
They have their own way of working.

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:38.160
And in the team that I work,

00:39:38.160 --> 00:39:40.320
the idea is that we are going to,

00:39:40.320 --> 00:39:41.440
we are doing things

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:43.840
differently than most of the teams.

00:39:44.800 --> 00:39:46.720
I am a really believer

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:48.400
that if you want a different results,

00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:49.760
you have to do things different.

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:52.320
And my manager is very aligned

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:53.280
with that as well.

00:39:53.360 --> 00:39:54.800
So we are doing things different

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:57.680
to prove that we can build software

00:39:57.680 --> 00:39:59.280
with a very high quality

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:00.880
and that our users

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:02.320
will love the software

00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:03.280
that they pay for.

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:07.490
So we can spread it

00:40:07.490 --> 00:40:08.770
to the rest of the company.

00:40:08.770 --> 00:40:12.450
So I am in this company

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:13.730
in my fourth month

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:16.370
and I'm still working just

00:40:16.370 --> 00:40:18.690
as the senior QA engineer

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:20.130
for the team I work on

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:21.810
until we prove

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:25.730
that we are getting

00:40:25.730 --> 00:40:27.170
the results that we want.

00:40:27.170 --> 00:40:28.450
So we are going to spread

00:40:29.390 --> 00:40:30.670
to the rest of the team

00:40:30.670 --> 00:40:32.350
and then I'm going to act more

00:40:32.350 --> 00:40:33.630
as the advisor

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:35.150
for the rest of the company.

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:38.830
We are actually getting started

00:40:38.830 --> 00:40:40.430
with this with some knowledge

00:40:40.430 --> 00:40:41.790
sharing sessions about the things

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:43.150
that we are doing differently

00:40:43.790 --> 00:40:45.790
and the results that we are getting

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:48.030
from doing these things differently.

00:40:48.030 --> 00:40:50.430
And when I say doing things differently

00:40:50.430 --> 00:40:52.990
is about using like for example

00:40:53.710 --> 00:40:56.750
most of the projects

00:40:56.750 --> 00:40:58.750
they use a test framework

00:40:58.750 --> 00:41:01.310
that was built in inside the company.

00:41:01.310 --> 00:41:03.870
We decided to go with a test framework

00:41:03.870 --> 00:41:05.870
that is a standard in the industry.

00:41:06.590 --> 00:41:08.350
And when I joined

00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:09.470
we had no tests.

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:11.630
